353 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 155 0 R
/E (This analysis provides strong evidence in support of the policy changes proposed in House File 3902 and Senate File 4044, including repealing the SHTC program's 2028 sunset provision and promoting greater geographic balance in funding allocations. Although the legislation was not passed in 2026, the findings of this report provide data-backed evidence to support these reforms.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
354 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 155 0 R
/E (Extend the State Housing Tax Credit Program Beyond 2028)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
355 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 155 0 R
/E (The last legislative session advocated for the extension of the Minnesota housing tax credit sunset, which was previously set to end after December 31, 2028.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
356 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 155 0 R
/E (The data analysis demonstrates strong and growing demand for the SHTC program from both affordable housing developers and contributors. Since its launch in 2023, annual allocations have reached the program cap increasingly quickly, with the entire 2026 allocation claimed in less than one day. Additionally, this data analysis suggests participation is driven largely by local small and mid-sized contributions, reflecting broad community support and demonstrating the program's success in leveraging private investment for affordable housing development )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
357 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 155 0 R
/E (Given the consistent oversubscription and strong public support, the 2028 sunset provision should be extended. This would provide long-term stability for developers and ensure continued investment in affordable housing across the state.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
358 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 155 0 R
/E (Promote Geographic Balance in SHTC Allocations)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
359 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 155 0 R
/E (This analysis also highlights the need for greater geographic balance in the distribution of SHTC funding. While numerous projects in Greater Minnesota have received SHTC support, they have received a substantially smaller share of total funding compared to projects located in the Twin Cities metropolitan area. As a result, rural communities that face some of the greatest housing development challenges often have less access to this valuable fund.  )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
360 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 155 0 R
/E (Establishing a proportional geographic allocation set-aside would ensure that rural and Greater Minnesota communities have equitable access to SHTC funding. )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
361 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 155 0 R
/E (Such a provision would support the development of affordable housing across all regions of the state. Geographic balance would strengthen the program's statewide impact and ensure that all Minnesotans can benefit from this locally generated investment in affordable housing.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
